     Utils:   BBS, Tmp, Allfiles
     Doors:   CDrom Door
     Cfg:     Allfiles.cfg.demo




   -Some new Tilde codes

    ~218   number of days since first log on of the current user
    ~219   number of days since last log on of the current user

   -New tilde code ~11 which causes a 1 second delay

    When you start a line with a ~11 code that line is printed
    1 second later then the line before this line.
    The same happens when you end a line with a ~11 code,
    because the delay is happens in a function which translates
    the line. When it encounters an ~11 code the translation
    is stopped for a second and when the translation is
    finished the line is displayed.

    So two ~11 codes in one line will delay the line with
    two seconds.


   -Worked a bit on the (spot) styling codes (*bold* etc.)
    it should work better now, because it would sometimes
    translate lines of ANSI drawings in messages.
    I was also working on a problem that it only uses
    one style at a time. But that wasn't finished
    when sending this update.


   -Added a new menu command "SetScanDays".

    Which does basically the same as "SetScanDate", it will set
    a new date to scan new files, but instead of asking for
    a date it asks for how many days you want to scan back.
    Something like this:

    Last New file scan was 1000 days ago at : 25-Feb-95 18:52:52
    How many days do you want to scan back ? : 999


   -The allfiles program can now also producs newfiles lists.
    For Allfiles add the next line to the part of the
    config where the allfiles is defined:

    TYPE ALLFILES

    For Newfiles add the next lines to the part of the
    config where the newfiles is defined.

    TYPE NEWFILES
    DAYS 14

    Note that the produced lists only contain validated files.


   -Changed the CDrom door , so you can type more then one digit
    when entering the disc number. This was needed to operate
    a system with more then 10 CDrom disks online.



   -Added an option to the mail processor to archive netmail,
    when you use the flag -N in packing mode netmail will
    be archived.
    Also made a little change so the names of archivers
    (in the systems.cfg and archiver.cfg can now be
    8 characters.

    Note that I set this up so netmail and echomail end
    up in separated archives.
    Only a precaution, because how larger an archive the bigger
    the change something goes wrong with it (and loose netmail).



   -It is possible to configure the mail processor so it
    can cooperate with the AmiGate program which gates
    newsgroups and E-mail .. AmiGate will convert the
    fido style archives to Inet style archives and
    put them in the UUSpool directory of the software
    that transfers mail with your provider.

    If you want to use this option all mail for the
    link has to be Archived, so you have to use the
    new "archive netmail" option of the mail processor.

    The program Amigate expects the fido style packets it needs
    to archive with a fixed name. But the mail processor
    makes packets and archives with a random name.
    This can be done by changing the archive.cfg

    When you add the folowing entry to the archive.cfg
    a script will be started to process the packet instead
    of an archiver. If you use this you have to have a
    corresponding node in the systems.cfg with UUCP
    as archiver instead of an archiver like LHA or LZX

    Example archive entry:


ARCHIVETYPE   "UUCP"
EXTENSION     ""
RECOGNIZE     FF FF FF FF
EXTRACT       ""
ARCHIVE       "execute trion:scripts/gatemail.scr %s"
#


    You also need a script in the Trion:Scripts directory
    to start the AmiGate program.
    What it does is renaming the packet to a fixed
    name and starting AmiGate .. which will process
    the packet and delete it.
    Also the script deletes the .flo files because
    the file would grow and never be deleted.

    With this script you would have something like this in
    you systems.cfg:

    2:283/326.999  UUCP  PASSWORD     ;Gate adress

    Example script:



.KEY  packet
;.DEF file=nix
.BRA  [
.KET  ]

rename [packet] trion:outbound/2.283.326.999.GAT
uucp:c/amigate gatepkt
delete trion:outbound/2.283.326.999.FLO



   -Also you can use this system to archive all the mail for
    one person and send it to them once in a while
    via Inet. Don't know if there are programs to actually
    automate the last bit but YamGate was mentioned
    for this purpose.

    Note that you need to add an entry to the archive.cfg
    for each archive with a unique name. You could use
    the first name of the person as archivername.
    Also you need a separate script for each system.

    A script for that use would look something like this:



.KEY  packet
;.DEF file=nix
.BRA  [
.KET  ]

lha a trion:outbound/username.lha [packet]
delete trion:outbound/2.283.326.100.FLO




